## 2.2 Canonical operators

### Controlled/existential viability

\[
\operatorname{Viab}^{T}
(\mathsf K;
\mathbb P)
=
\{x_0\in\mathsf K:
\exists\pi\in\mathbb P,
\ x^\pi(t)\in\mathsf K
\ \forall t\in T\}.
\]

### Robust/discriminating viability

\[
\operatorname{RViab}^{T}
(\mathsf K;
\mathbb P,\Delta)
=
\{x_0\in\mathsf K:
\exists\pi\in\mathbb P
\ \forall\delta\in\Delta,
\ x^{\pi,\delta}(t)\in\mathsf K
\ \forall t\in T\}.
\]

Use \(\operatorname{Disc}\) when specifically invoking discriminating-kernel game theory.

### Fixed-policy robust safety

\[
\operatorname{Inv}^{T}
(\mathsf K;\pi,\Delta)
=
\{x_0:\forall\delta\in\Delta,
 x^{\pi,\delta}(t)\in\mathsf K\}.
\]

### Epistemic viability

\[
\operatorname{EViab}^{T}
(\mathsf K;
\mathcal I,\mathbb P^{obs},\Delta)
\subseteq\mathsf B_q,
\]

where \(\mathsf B_q\) is an information/belief space.

### Institutional viability

Prefer either:

\[
\operatorname{IViab}^{T}
(\mathsf K;
\Gamma,\mathcal E,\mathbb P,\Delta)
\]

or a robust kernel with the institution-induced implementable policy class shown explicitly:

\[
\operatorname{RViab}^{T}
(\mathsf K;
\mathbb P_{impl}^{\mathfrak I},\Delta).
\]

### Chance viability

\[
\operatorname{ChViab}^{T,1-\epsilon}
(\mathsf K;\mathbb P,\mathbb P_d)
\]

requires an explicit probability law and filtration.

### Recovery

\[
\operatorname{Capt}^{H}_{\mathbb P,\Delta}
(C;\mathcal E)
\]

retains target, envelope, horizon, policy class, and disturbance class.

## 2.3 Inclusion rules

Literal inclusions require the same state space, safe set, horizon, information pattern, and aligned policy/disturbance classes.

Valid examples:

\[
\operatorname{Inv}(K;\pi,\Delta)
\subseteq
\operatorname{RViab}(K;\mathbb P,\Delta)
\subseteq
\operatorname{Viab}(K;\mathbb P).
\]

If stochastic realizations lie almost surely inside the robust disturbance class, then robust safety implies chance safety with probability one. It is **not** necessary that every robust disturbance have positive probability; the stochastic support merely must not include trajectories outside the robust class.

Epistemic kernels inhabit belief spaces. Compare them through a refinement map, common prior space, or physical projection—not bare set inclusion.

