# 3. Joint adjudication of the mathematical error docket

## A1 — Robust tangency versus strong invariance

### Joint verdict

**Diagnosis accepted.** The source must separate weak viability of an inclusion from strong invariance and one-policy/all-disturbance safety.

### Canonical repair

A safe sufficient statement is:

Let \(K\) be closed and let a feedback \(k\) satisfy

\[
F_k(x)
:=
\{f(x,k(x),d):d\in D(x)\}
\subseteq T_K^C(x)
\qquad\forall x\in K,
\]

under a strong-invariance theorem’s regularity and solution assumptions. Then every closed-loop disturbance solution remains in \(K\).

The weak condition

\[
F(x)\cap T_K(x)\ne\varnothing
\]

establishes only existential viability.

For the most general game semantics, use a discriminating kernel with explicitly ordered nonanticipative strategies. Do not claim a universal “if and only if” from pointwise tangency unless the exact strategy and regularity theorem supports it.

### Review-side correction

ER001’s strongest “iff” formulation and its strategy wording are more ambitious than justified by the packet. ER002/ER003’s conditional sufficient formulation is safer and should be canonical.

### Status

Conditional theorem after repair. High confidence.

---

## A2 — Order-minimal control

### Joint verdict

**Diagnosis accepted.** The correct result is existential viability under the order-best control.

### Repair

If minimum harvest produces an order-better trajectory than every admissible harvest and \(K\) is order-upward, then

\[
x_0\in\operatorname{Viab}(K)
\iff
\text{minimum-harvest trajectory from }x_0
\text{ remains in }K.
\]

It does not imply all higher-harvest paths are safe. A universal result would require safety under the order-worst/maximal harvest.

### Status

Theorem after wording correction.

