# 6. Restricted composition theorem target

## Jointly preferred sequence

### First theorem: bounded-interface robust invariance

Use Article 001’s restricted form:

- local closed safe sets;
- local policies that robustly preserve each set whenever interfaces remain within declared bounds;
- global coupling map proven to keep every interface within its tolerance on the product set;
- shared controls jointly feasible;
- event composition nonblocking.

Conclusion: product safe set is robustly invariant.

### Second theorem: gain-based extension

Add class-\(\mathcal K\) interface gains and a verified small-gain condition. Do not use a numeric spectral-radius matrix unless the gains have been reduced to linear coefficients. For nonlinear gains, use cyclic/nonlinear small-gain conditions.

### Required examples

- coupling destroys viability;
- coupling rescues viability;
- shared source/sink capacity makes local safe policies jointly infeasible.

### Reviewer-side correction

Some proposed external small-gain formulas treat nonlinear gain functions as matrix entries or assume the desired product invariant set. Those formulations are not adopted without a complete proof.

