# 10. Additional issues accepted from external reviews

## 10.1 Zeno and chattering in transformation

Valid. The first Operator II theorem should avoid Zeno through fixed review times or a one-transition-per-interval rule. General dwell-time/average-dwell-time extensions remain open.

## 10.2 RFDE kernels inhabit history spaces

Valid. Do not include RFDE kernels literally in ODE state-space kernels. Declare history spaces and projections.

## 10.3 HJI state-constraint boundary conditions

Valid only if the programme adopts HJI/viscosity computation. Then use a state-constraint viscosity solution framework and specify boundary conditions. It is not a prerequisite for the first set-valued/predecessor theorems.

## 10.4 Nonconvex disturbance relaxation gap

Valid. Convexification can introduce relaxed disturbance trajectories not realizable by ordinary signals. State convexity/relaxed-strategy assumptions and distinguish relaxed from ordinary kernels.

## 10.5 Joint typed safe set versus product of coordinate kernels

Valid and important. Typed verdicts do not imply

\[
\operatorname{RViab}
(K_P\cap K_F\cap K_N\cap K_R\cap K_E)
=
\bigcap_i\operatorname{RViab}(K_i)
\]

or a product identity under shared policies/disturbances. Official sustainability uses the **joint** robust kernel; typed components explain failure reasons.

## 10.6 Discontinuous feedback solution concept

Valid. Choose sample-and-hold as the default implementable semantics; use Filippov/Krasovskii or game strategies only when explicitly declared.

## 10.7 Finite-to-infinite horizon limit

Valid. Do not infer the infinite-horizon kernel merely by informal limit. State strict inwardness, compact nested predecessor conditions, or the exact convergence theorem.

## 10.8 Dynamic consistency of verdicts

Valid. Freeze \(\Omega\), define continuation specifications, and prevent status improvement by changing horizon or constraints mid-trajectory.

## 10.9 Specification-revision attacks

Valid. Operator II identity and obligation translation must constrain \(\mathcal R_A\); otherwise transformation becomes relabeling.

## 10.10 Robust-to-chance inclusion

Valid after correction: robust implies chance safety when stochastic realizations lie almost surely in the robust disturbance class. External reviewers proposing the opposite domination condition are not followed.

## 10.11 Comparison/antitheorem section

Valid editorial recommendation. The flagship should collect the central non-equivalences and counterexamples in one place.

## 10.12 Stochastic Operator II

Valid longer-term issue. Add a stochastic reach–avoid formulation only after the deterministic finite-mode theorem.

## 10.13 Bridge among sampled, continuous, and institutional kernels

Valid. Article 002 contains restricted pieces, not one general bridge theorem. Keep as explicit research programme.

